    "content": {
      "value": "Meeting Title: Booxi Appointment Integration — Website Journey Review\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Date and Time:** TBC (session was recorded)\n\n**Attendees:** - Gus Agra — Zimmermann (presenting) - Tessa Turnbull — Zimmermann - Emma Lawler — Zimmermann - Emma Kirwan — Zimmermann - Jasmijn Tsang — Accenture\n\n---\n\nAgenda Items\n------------\n\n### 1. Global Appointment Engine Overview\n\nGus walked through the global Booxi appointment journey. Booxi is the global appointment engine for all markets except China mainland, where WeCom is used. Hong Kong will use Booxi (not WeCom). There are three entry points on the website for booking appointments: My Account, the Appointment Landing Page, and the Store Locator. The PDP was intentionally excluded from scope for now — it is already in build, and there is a separate consideration around product reservation that would need to accompany it.\n\n### 2. My Account — Upcoming Appointments\n\n* Upcoming appointments will appear just below membership status. Only upcoming appointments will be shown (not past).\n* Clients can cancel or book a new appointment from this section.\n* Editing appointments is out of scope for now — it would disrupt the current retail team workflow.\n* Cancellation triggers a confirmation pop-up (UI still pending).\n* The footer of the appointments section will link to the Appointment Landing Page.\n* The upcoming appointments component requires API calls to Booxi to retrieve and cancel appointments.\n\n### 3. Appointment Landing Page\n\n* UI and content are still TBD.\n* The page will present two marketing-oriented reasons to book, each with a CTA that opens the native Booxi (iframe).\n* Emma Lawler raised whether the two CTAs represent different booking types with different durations. Gus clarified the split is more persuasive/marketing messaging than distinct service types, though it is technically possible to differentiate.\n* Agreed appointment length is 1 hour (returns may have had 30 minutes discussed, but not finalised).\n* Emma Lawler noted that having two distinct types would provide useful behavioural data.\n* Tessa raised the possibility of including sizing support as an appointment type — no decision made.\n\n### 4. CA-Generated Appointments (Client Services)\n\n* Two paths exist: client-generated and CA-generated.\n* Client Advisors and Client Services can book appointments on behalf of clients.\n* Store managers will have a view of upcoming appointments and can assign a CA to each one.\n\n### 5. Store Locator\n\n* Each store card will have a \"Book an Appointment\" CTA, which opens the native Booxi.\n* For China mainland stores, clicking the CTA will open a modal with instructions to use WeCom to book — rather than linking out to WeCom directly.\n\n### 6. China / WeCom Scope\n\n* The upcoming appointments section in My Account will call Booxi only — WeCom integration is future scope if needed.\n* China should be ready for the WeCom rollout by Phase 2 (19 Oct – early Nov). If not ready, the WeCom modal can be removed.\n\n### 7. Timelines\n\n* Clienteling build to finalise with Booxi integration first.\n* Pilot in Sydney stores: early October.\n* Global rollout (including China): Phase 2, 19 Oct – early Nov. If pilot issues arise, rollout pushed to February.\n* Website integration: Phase 3 — ideally March. No dependency on the website for the store rollout; website is an additional booking channel.\n\n### 8. Booxi UI Customisation\n\n* The Booxi pop-up is an iframe. Limited CSS customisation is available (basic colours). Gus to connect the team with Booxi to understand full customisation options.\n\n---\n\nAction Items\n------------\n\n| Task | Owner | Due Date | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Share Booxi integration documentation | Gus Agra | TBC | Emma Kirwan requested this to support website integration planning |\n| Follow up with Tash re: website appointment work — to be included in next phase planning | Emma Kirwan | TBC | Work needs to start prior to go live in March |\n| Connect Accenture/web team with Booxi re: iframe CSS customisation options | Gus Agra | TBC |  |\n| Confirm appointment type split (1 hour vs 30 min for returns) and whether two distinct types will be used | TBC | TBC | Emma Lawler flagged behavioural data value of splitting types |\n| Confirm WeCom readiness for China rollout | TBC | Before 19 Oct | If not ready, WeCom modal can be excluded |\n\n---\n\nNext Steps\n----------\n\n* Gus to share Booxi API/integration documentation.\n* Emma Kirwan to follow up with Tash to begin planning website appointment work (Phase 3, target March).\n* Website integration is not a dependency for the October pilot or global rollout — it runs in parallel.\n\n---\n\nOther Notes\n-----------\n\n* The Booxi iframe allows basic colour/style overrides but customisation is limited. A direct conversation with Booxi is recommended to scope what's possible before UI design is finalised.\n* No decision was made on sizing support as an appointment type — worth a follow-up with the business.",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "1f4dd",
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

